From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wsclean-mx <wsclean-mx@localhost>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 08:49:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 12/27] Metal: pull-model gridding variant (polycols), measured
 and scan-bound

Gives every grid cell exactly one owner: one cell per thread, one scalar
accumulator, a 32x32 threadgroup, no atomics and no indexing tricks, with the
same placement and weights as the push variants. This is the direct answer to
the 16x gridding/degridding gap that the real-data measurement exposed.

It is correct - within 1e-7 of the float64 twin at grid 64/128/512/1024, and it
reproduces the push variants' own rel_max (2.11e-05 at 50.4, 1.17e-06 at 8
lambda/cell) on the 440 745 real rows, so two independent accumulation
strategies cross-check each other - and it is 4.8-5.1x SLOWER: 160.7 ms vs
31.6 ms at 50.4, 84.9 ms vs 17.6 ms at 8.

Cause: every thread walks the whole tile bucket, so the cost is
sum over tiles of (cells) x (visibilities touching the tile) ~ 10^9 iterations
for clustered data. The fix is finer binning (per tile and column group), not
kernel arithmetic; with 8 groups the same model predicts ~20 ms and ~11 ms.

Two probe fixes fell out of this, both of which faked a kernel failure:
* a poly variant missing from the polynomial-reference list was compared against
  the NORMALISED analytic reference, which reads as a ~4x error in an exact
  kernel;
* tie test visibilities at +-63.5 cells are outside any grid smaller than ~136,
  which crashed 64/128 runs and made them look like kernel bugs.
Also adds --variant poly / polyh, which did not exist (the poly variants were
only reachable through --variant all).

